[Narrator]
As you lie in bed, you are stirred awake by an ocean of murmurs.{music 5-tension.ogg}

The sound swallows you whole and pulls you under.

You didn't really think you could get away from us that easily?

Your eyes snap open to the sight of monstrous faces in the shadows.

They grow more vivid and grotesque by the second.

They gather all around your bed.

They reach out to suffocate you.

[Pizza]
MIAU!{slideshow bedroom.slideshow}

[Narrator]
{music 6-goodmorning.ogg}Over the next week, the two of you remain inseparable.

Pizza, needy as ever, sends you on errand after errand, but you don't complain.

It is nice, a privilege even, to be needed.

And besides, he serves as a great excuse to get your act together.

In cleaning up behind him, you've started to clean up after yourself as well.

Your place isn't spotless by any stretch of the imagination, but you look forward to coming home to neatness.

To Pizza.

To a positive kind of consistency.

Oh yeah, that's right, you started to leave the house again.

At first it was just for a litter box and some proper cat food.

Now, though, you're going out for yourself.

Just short, brisk walks around the block, but they do make a difference.

It feels as though colour has reentered your life.

The soft sing-song sound of birds drifts dreamily through your half-open bedroom window, waking you gently.

Sunlight filters in through the gap between the curtains, warming the parts of your body blanketed by its rays.

It's a good day to have a good day.

But something's off. Pizza isn't lying on you. He isn't snuggled up at the foot of the bed either. 

This isn't like him.

[Jet]
Pizza? Piiizzzzzaaaaa?

[Narrator]
No answer. 

You scan your room for any sign of him, but are interrupted by a strange noise coming from the front of the house.

Scrrrrk... scrrrrk...

[Cat]
Mrrrowwwww

[Narrator]
You find Pizza pawing at the door as he looks to you for help.

The doorbell rings suddenly.

Ding-dong

From the peephole, you see a young lady, mid-twenties at most, pacing up and down your porch.

You recognize her from her frizzy jet-black hair and large, rounded glasses. She lives just a few houses down from you.

You used to see her at the grocer's from time to time or when getting mail before... well, before. But you've never really spoken to her properly.

She stops, walks closer to your door, and kneels in front of it.

[Lady]
Psst psst psst, Pizzaaaaa. Don't you worry, I'm here; I'm not going nowhere.

[Narrator]
Pizza looks at you expectantly.

[Pizza]
Miau?

[Narrator]
It's been ages since you spoke to someone properly. 

The cashiers at the store don't count. 

But what choice do you really have right now?

You take a deep breath, reach out slowly, and open the door.

Pizza darts out immediately to cozy up against the woman, smooshing his face into her hands and licking at them repeatedly.

[Lady]
Awwww, Pizza! Baby, look at you! I've been searching E V E R Y W H E R E for you. 

Had me worried sick. Don't you ever do that again...

[Narrator]
Immediately, Pizza puts on the poor lost kitty act as he rolls on his back and shows her his stomach.

[Lady]
Hmm, seems like someone's been eating well, huh?

[Narrator]
The woman rises to meet your gaze, dusting off her knees with a grateful smile.

[Jet]
I... had no idea he was yours... didn’t even know you had a cat, if I did I would hav–

[Lady]
Oh, no no no, don't apologize; please, if anything, I should be thanking you for taking care of my baby. 

Pizza's a handful and a half. I hope he didn't give you too much trouble.

[Jet]
No, no trouble at all, actually.

[Pizza]
Mrowww.

[Narrator]
Pizza leaves the woman's side and reenters your apartment to sit by you.

[Lady]
Whoa, he usually hates new people. Must really like you!

[Jet]
...

[Lady]
...

[Pizza]
...

[Narrator]
...

[Lady]
You're not too great at small talk, huh?

[Pizza]
Not really...

[Lady]
I guess this goofball doesn't want to leave just yet.

Mind if I come in?

[Narrator]
You glance back at your apartment.

It is neat, orderly even. But it could be better.

[Pizza]
Meowww.

[Narrator]
It could be. Yet it isn't. And that's just fine.

It'll get there eventually.

For now, though, it's good enough.

[Jet]
Yes.

[Lady]
Yes, you mind?

[Jet]
No, yes... as in no.

*Sigh*

Come in, please make yourself at home.

...{slideshow end.slideshow}

